Yes, I’m aware of Obelisks — a recently discovered class of viroid-like RNA elements that colonize human microbiomes, including the mouth.

What they are
Obelisks are tiny circular RNA molecules (roughly 1,000 nucleotides long) that fold into distinctive rod-like (obelisk-shaped) secondary structures. They were identified in 2024 through computational analysis of vast metatranscriptomic datasets. Unlike classic viruses, they lack a protein coat. Unlike plant viroids, their sequences are completely novel and they encode at least one previously unknown protein family called Oblins. Some also contain hammerhead ribozyme motifs that help with self-cleavage/replication.

They form their own phylogenetic group with no detectable sequence similarity to known viruses, viroids, or other biological agents.

Presence in the mouth
Obelisks are notably more common in the oral microbiome than in the gut:
Detected in roughly 50% of oral (saliva/mouth) metatranscriptome samples examined.
Detected in only about 7% of stool/gut samples.
One specific Obelisk (Obelisk-S.s) is hosted by Streptococcus sanguinis, a common commensal bacterium that lives in the human mouth and dental plaque. Lab studies show this Obelisk can persist in the bacterium but is not essential for its growth under normal conditions.

They can remain in the same individual for extended periods (observed >300 days in at least one case). Different Obelisk types appear to show some anatomical preference (oral vs. gut).

“Buildup” and health effects
There is currently no evidence of pathological “buildup,” accumulation that causes disease, or any clear impact on human health — positive or negative. Researchers describe them as colonists of the microbiome whose biological roles, life cycles, and effects (if any) remain unknown. Their discovery is still relatively new (preprint 2024, peer-reviewed follow-ups ongoing), so the science is early-stage.

They are natural RNA elements already present in many people’s mouths and guts; they are not related to mRNA vaccines, synthetic mRNA, or any pharmaceutical product.

Relation to the broader oral discussion
Because they reside in oral bacteria (including plaque-associated species like S. sanguinis), the multi-pass hygiene routine you’ve described — antiseptic mouthwash scrubs, dedicated tongue cleaning, flossing, and thorough mechanical removal of debris — would be expected to influence the overall oral microbial environment in which these elements live. Whether that meaningfully changes Obelisk prevalence or activity is not yet studied.

In short: Obelisks are a real, newly recognized class of RNA entities that are relatively common in human mouths, hosted at least in part by ordinary oral bacteria. Their significance for digestion, inflammation, or systemic health is still an open scientific question.
